REGENT OF EAST HALMAHERA

NORTH MALUKU PROVINCE

REGIONAL REGULATION OF EAST HALMAHERA REGENCY

NUMBER 7 OF 2022

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF EAST HALMAHERA,

Considering:
a. that women and children have the right to obtain a sense of security and freedom from all forms of torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and honor;
b. that all forms of violence against women and children constitute violations of human rights so that their dignity and honor need to be protected and their right to life guaranteed in accordance with their nature and disposition without discrimination;
c. that based on Article 12 of Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government, women's empowerment and child protection are mandatory governmental affairs for regional governments;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a, b, and c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation of East Halmahera Regency concerning Protection of Women and Children;

In View of:
1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;

2. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
3. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of all forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
4. Law Number 20 of 1999 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention Number 138 Concerning Minimum Age FOR Admission to Employment (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 56, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3835);
5. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
6. Law Number 1 of 2000 concerning Ratification of ILO Convention Number 182 Concerning The Prohibition and Immediate Action For the Elimination of the worst Forms of Child Labour (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 30, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3941);
7. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2000 Number 208, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as last amended by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);
8. Law Number 1 of 2003 concerning Establishment of North Halmahera Regency, South Halmahera Regency, Sula Islands Regency, East Halmahera Regency and Tidore Islands in North Maluku Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 21, Supplement to the State Gazette Number 4264);
9. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
10. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635);

11 . Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);

12. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4967);
13. Law Number 25 of 2009 concerning Public Services (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2009 Number 112 , Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5038);

Hereby Decides:

14. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning Formation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234);
15. Law Number 10 of 2012 concerning Ratification of the Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of the Child on the Sale of Children, Child Prostitution and Child Pornography (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 149, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5330);
16. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679);
17. Law Number 30 of 2014 concerning Government Administration (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 292, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5601);
18. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence, (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);

With Mutual Approval

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

Protection of Women and Children, is implemented based on the principles of:
a. respect and protection of Human Rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of the Child;
e. right to life, survival, and development;
f. empowerment; and g. legal certainty.

Article 3

This Regional Regulation aims to:
a. guarantee the fulfillment of the rights of women and children;
b. maintain the integrity of the household in order to realize a harmonious family;
c. prevent violence against women and children;
d. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children;
e. provide services to women and children victims of acts of violence;
f. provide protection to reporters and witnesses; and g. empower women victims of violence.

Article 4

Every Woman has the right: a to life and the right to maintain life and livelihood;
b to form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage;

c to develop herself through the fulfillment of her basic needs;
d to obtain education and benefit from science and technology, arts and culture, in order to improve the quality of her life and for the welfare of mankind;

e to advance herself in fighting for her rights collectively to build society, nation and state;

f to recognition, guarantee, protection and fair legal certainty as well as equal treatment before the law;

g to work and receive fair and proper remuneration and treatment in employment relations;

h to obtain equal opportunities in government;
i to freedom of association, assembly and expression of opinion;
j to protection of self, family, honor, dignity and property under her control, and the right to a sense of security and protection from threats of fear to do or not do something that is a basic right;

k to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and the right to obtain political asylum from other countries;
l to live in prosperity physically and mentally, to reside and obtain a good and healthy living environment, and the right to obtain health services;

m to social security that enables her full development as a dignified human being;

n to equality of her position in law and government; o to decent work and livelihood for humanity;

p in the defense of the state;

q to communicate and obtain information to develop her personal and social environment, and the right to seek, obtain, possess, store, process, and convey information using all types of available channels;
r to embrace her respective religion and to worship according to that religion and belief; and s to obtain education.

Article 7

Implementation of women's empowerment in the economic field as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (2) is carried out through: a provision of skills and job training;

b facilitation of the formation of productive economic business groups;
c facilitation of strengthening and development of productive economic business groups;

d facilitation and capital assistance; and e facilitation of marketing network development.

Article 9

(1) The implementation of women's empowerment in the political field as referred to in Article 5 paragraph (2) includes: a involvement of women in decision-making at various levels;
b provision of opportunities for women to hold public office;

c participation in general elections; and d self-development through organizations to associate, assemble and express opinions.

2. The implementation of women's empowerment in the political field as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out in accordance with applicable laws and regulations.

Article 11

(1) To implement Women's Empowerment as referred to in Article 6, the Regional Government shall organize gender mainstreaming including: a establishing the implementation of gender mainstreaming;

b coordinating, facilitating, and mediating gender mainstreaming;

c facilitating institutional strengthening and development of gender mainstreaming mechanisms in government institutions, women's study centers, research and development institutions, non-governmental institutions;

d implementing gender mainstreaming related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

e improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

f integrating efforts to improve the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

g coordinating the implementation of improving the quality of life of women related to the fields of development, education, health, economy, law, Human Rights and politics, environment and socio-culture;

h organizing the protection of women, especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;

i facilitating the integration of protection of women, especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities, in conflict areas and disaster-affected areas;

j coordinating the protection of women, especially protection for victims of violence, female workers, elderly women and women with disabilities;

k facilitating the strengthening and development of networks of community institutions or organizations and the business sector for the implementation of gender mainstreaming;

1 developing a gender-based information system; and m providing and organizing integrated or non-integrated services for women victims of violence, female workers, elderly women, and women with disabilities.

2. Further provisions regarding the forms of women's empowerment and gender mainstreaming mechanisms as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 13

Every child has the right to obtain:
a. birth registration;
b. health;
c. education; andd. social welfare.

Article 14

Every child has the obligation to:
a. respect parents, guardians and teachers;
b. love family, community and care for friends;
c. love the homeland, nation and state;
d. perform worship in accordance with their religion and beliefs;
e. study and develop themselves in accordance with their abilities and talents, interests; andf. have noble character.

Article 23

For children who do not pursue formal education, they may pursue education through non-formal education units comprising:
a For children who are 14 (fourteen) years of age and above and do not pursue formal elementary school education or equivalent may pursue non-formal Package A education or equivalent to Elementary School (SD)/Madrasah Ibtidaiyah (MI);

b For children who are 17 (seventeen) years of age and above and do not pursue formal junior high school education or equivalent may pursue non-formal Package B education or equivalent to Junior High School (SMP) / Madrasah Tsanawiyah (MTs); or c For children who are 21 (twenty-one) years of age and above and do not pursue formal junior high school education or equivalent may pursue non-formal Package C education or equivalent to Senior High School (SMA)/Vocational High School (SMK)/Madrasah Aliyah/Madrasah Aliyah Kejuruan (MAK);

Article 24

1. The implementation of Protection and empowerment of children in the field of social welfare as referred to in Article 13 letter d, the Regional Government is obliged to organize social welfare for:
a. children in conflict with the law;
b. child victims of violence who are exploited economically and sexually, neglected children, child victims of kidnapping and child victims of trafficking in persons;
c. child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substance abuse (NAPZA);
d. children infected with HIV/ AIDS;
e. children who do not have parents and are cared for by other parties or families who are unable;
f. street children;
g. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters;
h. children with disabilities;
1. children of migrant worker families;

J. children living in or around prostitution locations; and  k. child victims of other maltreatment.

2. Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of service provision: a health;

b education;

c legal aid;

d assistance;

e social, mental and spiritual guidance;

f social rehabilitation;

g empowerment;

h social assistance; and/ or 1 reintegration.

Article 25

1. The Regional Government is obliged to protect the fulfillment of the rights of child workers in the informal sector.
2. Child Workers in the informal sector as referred to in paragraph (1) comprise: a domestic workers;

b shoe shiners;

c street vendors;

d scavengers;

e parking attendants;

f children working in agriculture, livestock and plantations;  g children working in mining; and h other informal sector work that employs children.  (3) Every person who employs children in the informal sector is obliged to observe the requirements: a minimum age of 15 (fifteen) years, except for the Worst Forms of Child Labor category minimum 18 (eighteen) years;  b obtain written consent from the parents/guardian of the child worker;  c have a written employment agreement between the employer and the parents/guardian of the child worker and obtain validation from the authorized agency;

d not employed at night;

e working time of at most 3 (three) hours per day;

f not employed in a place or environment that can interfere with the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the child's health;

g provide opportunities to obtain education in accordance with their talents and interests;

h employ for light types of work; and  1 provide one day off per week.

Article 26

Fulfillment of Children's Rights as referred to in Article 25 in the form of:
a counseling to stakeholders about children's rights;  b psychological, medical and legal assistance services and socio-economic reintegration;

c family empowerment through provision of training, business capital stimulus and assistance;

d scholarships to continue formal education to higher levels; and e non-formal education and skills training for those who do not pursue formal education.

Article 27

Forms of violence comprise:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and/ or f. other violence.

Article 30

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 27 letter c is caused by:
a. acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. forced sexual intercourse;
c. forced sexual intercourse in an unnatural or unwanted manner;  and/or d. forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.

Article 31

Neglect as referred to in Article 27 letter d is caused by:
a. acts that result in the non-fulfillment of children's needs properly, whether physical, mental, spiritual or social carried out by parents, guardians, or any other party responsible for their care;
b. acts of deliberately neglecting to maintain, care for, or take care of children as they should be carried out by parents, guardians, or other parties responsible for their care;
c. acts of neglecting a person within their household scope, whereas according to the law applicable to them or because of an agreement or contract they are obliged to provide livelihood, care, or maintenance to that person; and/ or d. acts that result in economic dependence by restricting and/ or prohibiting proper work inside or outside the home so that the victim is under the control of that person.

Article 34

1. Efforts to prevent violence against women and children shall be carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government and coordinated by the technical Regional Apparatus.
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be implemented by:a. conducting guidance towards harmonious household integrity;
b. forming work networks in efforts to prevent violence;
c. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
d. establishing a violence prevention system;
e. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence; andf. providing critical education regarding the rights of women and children to the community.

Article 35

In addition to the Regional Government, prevention efforts as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (1) shall be carried out by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions; andd. Non-Governmental Organizations.

Article 36

The implementation of handling of women and children victims of violence shall be carried out with:
a. speed;
b. safety and comfort;
c. sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easy accessibility;
f. no charges; andg. guaranteed confidentiality.

Article 37

Forms of handling of women and children victims of violence include:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. medical rehabilitation services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal services; andf. social reintegration services.

Article 39

Assistance services as referred to in Article 37 letter b include:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;
b. accompanying victims during the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
c. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the police, prosecutor's office and court;
d. protecting the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including reporting by mass media;
e. providing a sense of security to victims; andf. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 42

Legal services as referred to in Article 37 letter e are to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process by:  a. providing legal consultation that includes information regarding the rights of victims and the judicial process;
b. accompanying victims at the investigation, prosecution, and examination levels in court hearings and assisting victims to fully describe the violence they experienced; andc. coordinating with fellow law enforcers, volunteer assistants, and social workers so that the judicial process proceeds as it should.

Article 44

1. The Regional Government in organizing the protection of women and children from acts of violence, shall establish P2TP2A as a regional technical implementing unit (UPTDD) integrated into the Technical Regional Apparatus.
2. The organization of protection and services for women and children victims of violence shall be carried out in an integrated manner by P2TP2A.  (3) The organization of services for women and children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:a. providing fast and free services to victims;
b. organizing protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance;
c. cooperating with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants needed for victims;
d. networking with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of victims and cooperating with witness and victim protection institutions, social shelters or trauma centers owned by the government, community or other institutions for health recovery of victims;
e. providing convenience, comfort and safety for victims;
f. maintaining the confidentiality of victims; andg. providing fulfillment of legal assistance for victims.
4. Further provisions regarding the establishment, procedures and mechanisms for organizing P2TP2A shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER V

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

Article 45

Every woman and child victim of violence shall receive the following rights:
a. right to complaint handling;
b. right to have their dignity respected as human beings;
c. right to receive protection from family, community, regional government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on protection determination from the court;
d. right to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by victims;
e. right to determine their own decisions;
f. right to special handling related to victim confidentiality;
g. right to obtain information;
h. right to confidentiality of identity;
1. right to social rehabilitation;

J. right to assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with laws and regulations; and  k. right to spiritual guidance services.

Article 46

Women and children victims of violence in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 45, shall also receive the following special rights:  a. right to survival;
b. right to grow and develop;
c. right to receive equal protection; andd. right to participate in community life.

CHAPTER VI

PROHIBITIONS

Article 47

(1) Every business operation whose activities may disrupt the growth and development of children is prohibited from receiving child visitors.  (2) Business operations as referred to in paragraph (1) include:  a discotheque;

b night club;

c bar;

d adult karaoke;

e pub;

f massage parlor;

g steam bath/sauna parlor; and h other similar business fields.

Article 48 Every operator of hotels, motels, inns, tourism guesthouse businesses and similar business activities is prohibited from renting rooms to children without being accompanied by parents or adult family members or supervising teachers/persons in charge in the context of carrying out school activities or other activities.
